// Domain 5: Data Protection (18%)
// Schema: id, d (domain), q ("scenario\n\nquestion"), o (options), a (indices of correct options), r (one rationale per option).
// Options are shuffled at runtime, so rationales never refer to option letters.
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d5-01", d: 5,
  q: "A public web application runs behind an Application Load Balancer that has an HTTP listener on port 80. A new policy requires that all client connections use HTTPS with TLS 1.2 or later, and that users who type an http:// address still reach the site.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add an HTTPS listener with the ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06 security policy. Change the HTTP listener to redirect to HTTPS.",
      "Add an HTTPS listener with the oldest predefined security policy. Keep the HTTP listener forwarding to the targets for older clients.",
      "Remove the HTTP listener, and configure the application on the targets to reject any connection that does not use TLS 1.2 or later.",
      "Add an AWS WAF rule that blocks all requests on port 80. Ask users to update their bookmarks to use https:// for the site."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06 policy supports TLS 1.3 and TLS 1.2 only. A redirect action on the HTTP listener sends users who use http:// to the HTTPS listener.",
      "Older security policies allow TLS 1.0 and 1.1, and forwarding HTTP keeps unencrypted traffic.",
      "Without an HTTP listener, users who type http:// cannot reach the site, and the load balancer, not the target, negotiates TLS with clients.",
      "Blocking port 80 breaks access for users who type http://, which the requirement does not allow."] },

{ id: "d5-02", d: 5,
  q: "A security review finds that some applications send requests to a sensitive Amazon S3 bucket over plain HTTP. The bucket must reject every request that does not use HTTPS.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy statement that denies all S3 actions when the aws:SecureTransport condition key is false.",
      "Turn on default encryption with SSE-KMS for the bucket. Use a customer managed key for every object.",
      "Turn on S3 Block Public Access for the bucket. Remove any public grants from the object ACLs.",
      "Add a CORS configuration to the bucket that allows requests only from origins that start with https:// links."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["aws:SecureTransport is false when a request is sent without TLS. A deny statement on that condition rejects every HTTP request to the bucket.",
      "Default encryption protects data at rest. It does not control whether requests use TLS.",
      "Block Public Access controls public access, not the transport that callers use.",
      "CORS applies to browser cross-origin requests and does not block HTTP requests from applications."] },

{ id: "d5-03", d: 5,
  q: "A company's Amazon S3 bucket already rejects requests that do not use HTTPS. A new compliance rule requires that clients use TLS 1.2 or later. Requests that use TLS 1.0 or TLS 1.1 must be denied.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add a bucket policy statement that denies all S3 actions when the s3:TlsVersion condition key is less than 1.2.",
      "Add a bucket policy statement that denies all S3 actions when the aws:SecureTransport condition key is set to true.",
      "Turn on S3 Transfer Acceleration for the bucket, because it supports only TLS 1.2 or later from all clients.",
      "Ask each client team to upgrade its SDK. Review the S3 server access logs each month for any old TLS use."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The s3:TlsVersion condition key contains the TLS version of the request. A deny statement with a numeric condition for values less than 1.2 rejects TLS 1.0 and 1.1.",
      "This would deny every HTTPS request, which is the opposite of the goal.",
      "Transfer Acceleration changes the network path, not the TLS versions that the bucket accepts.",
      "Asking teams to upgrade does not deny old TLS versions."] },

{ id: "d5-04", d: 5,
  q: "A company serves a web application through Amazon CloudFront with an Application Load Balancer as the origin. Traffic must be encrypted from the viewer to CloudFront and from CloudFront to the origin. Viewers that use HTTP must be sent to HTTPS.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Set the viewer protocol policy on the cache behaviors to Redirect HTTP to HTTPS.",
      "Set the origin protocol policy to HTTPS only, with a valid certificate on the origin.",
      "Set the viewer protocol policy to HTTP and HTTPS for all viewers.",
      "Set the origin protocol policy to HTTP only on port 80 of the origin.",
      "Turn on field-level encryption for every field in the requests that viewers send."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["The Redirect HTTP to HTTPS viewer protocol policy sends HTTP viewers to HTTPS, so traffic between viewers and CloudFront is encrypted.",
      "The HTTPS only origin protocol policy makes CloudFront connect to the origin over HTTPS, so traffic to the origin is encrypted too.",
      "Allowing HTTP leaves viewer traffic unencrypted.",
      "HTTP only to the origin leaves the second part of the path unencrypted, and caching does not need HTTP.",
      "Field-level encryption protects specific fields. It does not encrypt the whole connection."] },

{ id: "d5-05", d: 5,
  q: "A company exposes an API to partner systems through an Application Load Balancer. Each partner has a client certificate issued by the company's certificate authority. The load balancer must accept connections only from clients that present a valid certificate from that authority.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Configure mutual TLS on the HTTPS listener in verify mode, with a trust store that holds the company's CA certificate.",
      "Add an AWS WAF rule that allows requests only when an X-Client-Certificate header is present in each of the partner requests.",
      "Configure the HTTPS listener with the newest TLS security policy. Give each partner an API key for the requests.",
      "Add a security group rule that allows only the partners' IP addresses. Keep server-only TLS on the HTTPS listener."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["In verify mode, the load balancer performs mutual TLS authentication and checks each client certificate against the CA certificates in the trust store. Clients without a valid certificate are rejected.",
      "A header can be set by anyone and is not certificate authentication.",
      "A TLS policy controls protocol versions and ciphers, and API keys are not certificate authentication.",
      "IP rules do not check certificates, and partner IP addresses can change."] },

{ id: "d5-06", d: 5,
  q: "A public certificate from AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) on an Application Load Balancer was validated with DNS validation a year ago. ACM now reports that the certificate is ineligible for automatic renewal. Last month, the DNS team cleaned up old records in the domain's hosted zone.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause of this issue?",
  o: ["The CNAME record that ACM uses for DNS validation was removed, so ACM cannot validate the domain to renew.",
      "ACM public certificates cannot be renewed automatically and must be imported again each year by the team.",
      "The certificate is attached to a load balancer, and ACM renews only certificates that are not in use at all.",
      "The certificate was issued in the wrong Region, and ACM renews certificates only in the us-east-1 Region."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["ACM renews DNS-validated certificates automatically as long as the validation CNAME record stays in DNS. If the record is removed, ACM cannot confirm control of the domain, so the team must add the record back.",
      "ACM renews its public certificates automatically when validation still works.",
      "ACM renews certificates that are in use with integrated services such as load balancers.",
      "Certificates in any Region can renew. The us-east-1 rule applies to certificates used with CloudFront."] },

{ id: "d5-07", d: 5,
  q: "A company runs internal services that communicate over TLS. The services need certificates for private DNS names such as orders.internal.example.com. The certificates must renew automatically, and the company does not want to run its own certificate authority software.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create a CA in AWS Private Certificate Authority. Request private certificates for the services through ACM.",
      "Request public certificates from ACM for the private DNS names. Validate each name with email validation.",
      "Run an open source certificate authority on Amazon EC2, and renew each of the certificates with a scheduled script.",
      "Create self-signed certificates on each server. Copy each certificate to every client by hand each year."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Private CA is a managed private certificate authority. ACM can issue private certificates from it and renew them automatically, with no CA software to run.",
      "Public certificate authorities cannot issue certificates for private internal names.",
      "Running CA software on EC2 is the work that the company wants to avoid.",
      "Self-signed certificates must be distributed and renewed by hand."] },

{ id: "d5-08", d: 5,
  q: "A company processes sensitive data with Apache Spark on Amazon EMR. Data that moves between the cluster's nodes, and between the cluster and Amazon S3, must be encrypted in transit. The local disks of the nodes must be encrypted at rest.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an EMR security configuration that turns on in-transit encryption with TLS certificates and local disk encryption. Use it for the cluster.",
      "Turn on default encryption on the S3 buckets that the cluster reads from and writes to. Launch the cluster with no other encryption settings.",
      "Run the cluster in a private subnet with no internet gateway, because traffic inside a VPC is always encrypted by AWS at every network layer.",
      "Encrypt the source data with client-side encryption, and decrypt the data on each node before Spark processes it, with no cluster security configuration."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["EMR security configurations set encryption for a cluster. In-transit encryption uses TLS certificates that you provide for the open source frameworks, and local disk encryption protects the nodes' disks.",
      "Bucket encryption protects S3 data at rest. It does not encrypt traffic between nodes or the local disks.",
      "A private subnet controls routing, and it does not guarantee encryption between all instance types.",
      "Data decrypted on the nodes still moves between them without encryption."] },

{ id: "d5-09", d: 5,
  q: "A data science team runs distributed training jobs in Amazon SageMaker AI across several instances. The training data is sensitive, and the data that moves between the training containers during a job must be encrypted.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Turn on inter-container traffic encryption for the training jobs.",
      "Turn on default encryption for the S3 bucket that holds the data.",
      "Run the training jobs on a single instance with no network access.",
      "Encrypt the model artifacts with an AWS KMS key after each job."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Inter-container traffic encryption encrypts the traffic between the containers in a distributed training job.",
      "Bucket encryption protects data at rest in S3, not traffic between containers.",
      "A single instance avoids the traffic but does not meet the need for distributed training.",
      "Encrypting artifacts after the job does not protect data in transit during the job."] },

{ id: "d5-10", d: 5,
  q: "A company runs a legacy application on Amazon EC2 instances that talk to each other over an unencrypted protocol inside one VPC. The application cannot be changed. The security team must encrypt the traffic between the instances.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Move the application to instance types that automatically encrypt traffic between supported Nitro instances.",
      "Rewrite the application to use TLS for all of the connections. Deploy the new version to all of the instances.",
      "Turn on VPC Flow Logs for the subnets. Alert when the logs show traffic that uses the unencrypted protocol.",
      "Set up an IPsec tunnel between each pair of instances by using software that the team installs and manages itself."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Some Nitro-based instance types automatically encrypt in-transit traffic between instances of supported types in the same VPC or a peered VPC, with no change to the application.",
      "The application cannot be changed.",
      "Flow logs record traffic but do not encrypt it.",
      "Self-managed IPsec tunnels between every pair of instances are a large operational burden."] },

{ id: "d5-11", d: 5,
  q: "Linux instances mount an Amazon EFS file system that stores regulated data. A security review requires that all NFS traffic between the instances and the file system is encrypted.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Mount the file system with the Amazon EFS mount helper and the tls mount option.",
      "Turn on encryption at rest for the file system. Mount it with standard options.",
      "Add a file system policy that denies all access from principals outside the VPC.",
      "Move the instances into the same Availability Zone as the file system's mount target."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The EFS mount helper with the tls option uses TLS to encrypt the NFS traffic between the client and the file system.",
      "Encryption at rest protects stored data, not data in transit.",
      "A file system policy controls access, not encryption. A policy with aws:SecureTransport could require TLS, but the clients would still need to mount with TLS.",
      "Being in the same Availability Zone does not encrypt the traffic."] },

{ id: "d5-12", d: 5,
  q: "A company stores logs in an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain. A security review requires that traffic between the domain's nodes is encrypted and that clients can connect to the domain only over HTTPS.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Turn on node-to-node encryption, and turn on the setting that requires HTTPS for all traffic to the domain.",
      "Turn on encryption at rest for the domain with an AWS KMS key. Keep the other network settings the same.",
      "Put the domain in a VPC, because traffic inside a VPC is encrypted between all nodes and all of the clients.",
      "Put an Application Load Balancer in front of the domain, and terminate TLS at the load balancer for clients."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Node-to-node encryption uses TLS for traffic between nodes. The require HTTPS setting rejects client requests that do not use HTTPS.",
      "Encryption at rest protects stored data, not traffic.",
      "A VPC controls network access. It does not encrypt traffic between nodes and clients.",
      "TLS that ends at a load balancer leaves traffic to the domain and between nodes unencrypted."] },

{ id: "d5-13", d: 5,
  q: "A security engineer changes an Application Load Balancer's security policy to ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-3-2021-06. Soon after, an older partner system can no longer connect, while modern browsers still work. The partner system supports TLS 1.2 but not TLS 1.3.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Change the listener to a policy that supports TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3, such as ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06.",
      "Change the listener to the oldest predefined security policy. Keep it in place for all clients.",
      "Add the partner system's IP address to the load balancer's security group. Keep the TLS 1.3 only policy.",
      "Replace the load balancer's certificate with a certificate that uses a longer key. Keep the current TLS 1.3 only policy."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The TLS13-1-3 policy accepts only TLS 1.3, so clients that support only TLS 1.2 fail. A policy that supports TLS 1.2 and 1.3 restores access without allowing older protocols.",
      "The oldest policies allow TLS 1.0 and 1.1, which weakens security more than needed.",
      "Network access is not the problem. The TLS handshake fails because of the protocol version.",
      "The certificate key length does not change which protocol versions the listener accepts."] },

{ id: "d5-14", d: 5,
  q: "A payment application must store its encryption keys in single-tenant hardware security modules (HSMs) that the company controls exclusively. The application uses the PKCS #11 interface to call the HSMs.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Use AWS CloudHSM, and have the application call the HSMs in the cluster through the PKCS #11 library.",
      "Use AWS KMS customer managed keys. Have the application call the KMS API through a PKCS #11 wrapper.",
      "Use AWS Secrets Manager to store the keys. Have the application retrieve them through PKCS #11 calls.",
      "Store the keys in an encrypted Amazon S3 bucket. Have the application download the keys at startup."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudHSM provides single-tenant HSMs in a cluster that the company controls. It supports the PKCS #11 library, as well as JCE and OpenSSL integrations.",
      "KMS uses multi-tenant HSMs that AWS manages, and it does not provide a PKCS #11 interface.",
      "Secrets Manager stores secrets, not keys in HSMs, and has no PKCS #11 interface.",
      "Keys downloaded from S3 are outside any HSM."] },

{ id: "d5-15", d: 5,
  q: "A company must encrypt Amazon S3 and Amazon EBS data with keys whose key material is generated and stored in single-tenant HSMs that the company controls. The company wants to keep using the native AWS KMS integration of these services.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create an AWS CloudHSM key store in AWS KMS backed by a CloudHSM cluster. Create KMS keys in it.",
      "Create KMS keys with imported key material. Keep a copy of the key material on a laptop safely.",
      "Use client-side encryption with keys from CloudHSM. Turn off server-side encryption in S3 and EBS.",
      "Create customer managed keys in AWS KMS with the default key store. Turn on automatic rotation."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An AWS CloudHSM key store is a custom key store that is backed by a CloudHSM cluster that the company controls. KMS keys in it work with KMS-integrated services such as S3 and EBS, while the key material stays in the cluster's HSMs.",
      "Imported key material is stored in KMS HSMs, not in single-tenant HSMs that the company controls.",
      "Client-side encryption gives up the native integration that the company wants to keep.",
      "The default key store uses KMS-managed HSMs, not single-tenant HSMs."] },

{ id: "d5-16", d: 5,
  q: "An application stores customer records in Amazon S3. A new rule requires that records are encrypted inside the application before they leave it, so that the data is never sent to AWS in plaintext. The keys must be managed in AWS KMS.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Use the AWS Encryption SDK with an AWS KMS keyring to encrypt the records in the application before upload.",
      "Turn on default bucket encryption with SSE-KMS by using a customer managed key for every object in the bucket.",
      "Turn on S3 Object Lock in compliance mode for the bucket to stop any other user from reading the records.",
      "Upload the records over HTTPS only. Add a bucket policy that denies requests that are sent without TLS."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The AWS Encryption SDK performs client-side envelope encryption. With a KMS keyring, data keys are protected by KMS keys, and the records are encrypted before they leave the application.",
      "SSE-KMS encrypts the data after S3 receives it, so S3 receives the plaintext over TLS.",
      "Object Lock prevents deletion and changes. It does not encrypt data.",
      "TLS protects the data in transit, but S3 still receives the plaintext."] },

{ id: "d5-17", d: 5,
  q: "An Amazon S3 bucket uses SSE-KMS with a customer managed key. The bucket receives millions of uploads each day, and the company's AWS KMS request costs are high. Some requests are throttled by the KMS request quota.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Turn on S3 Bucket Keys for the bucket's SSE-KMS default encryption.",
      "Change the bucket to use SSE-C with keys that the client application sends.",
      "Request a KMS quota increase. Keep the current encryption settings.",
      "Turn off encryption for the bucket. Encrypt the objects on the clients."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["S3 Bucket Keys create a bucket-level key that S3 uses to generate data keys, which reduces the requests from S3 to KMS. This lowers costs and the chance of throttling.",
      "SSE-C makes the clients manage and send keys, which adds work and risk.",
      "A higher quota does not reduce the request costs.",
      "Removing server-side encryption and moving it to clients adds work and weakens defaults."] },

{ id: "d5-18", d: 5,
  q: "A company must ensure that every new Amazon EBS volume created in its account in a Region is encrypted, including volumes created by teams that forget to select encryption.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Turn on EBS encryption by default for the account in the Region.",
      "Ask every team to select encryption each time it creates a volume.",
      "Run a nightly script that finds unencrypted volumes and deletes them.",
      "Create a CloudWatch alarm for each volume that the teams create there."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["EBS encryption by default makes the account encrypt all new volumes and snapshot copies in the Region, whatever the team selects.",
      "Relying on each team is what fails today.",
      "Deleting volumes loses data and does not prevent the problem.",
      "Alarms do not encrypt volumes."] },

{ id: "d5-19", d: 5,
  q: "An existing Amazon EC2 instance has an unencrypted Amazon EBS data volume. A security policy now requires the volume to be encrypted with a customer managed AWS KMS key.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create a snapshot, copy the snapshot with encryption and the KMS key, create a new volume from it, and replace the volume.",
      "Modify the existing volume, and turn on encryption with the KMS key while the volume stays attached to the instance.",
      "Turn on EBS encryption by default for the account, which encrypts the existing unencrypted volume within a day.",
      "Detach the volume, change its volume type to gp3, and attach it again. Turn on EBS encryption by default first."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An unencrypted volume cannot be encrypted in place. Copying a snapshot with encryption, or creating a volume from the snapshot with encryption, gives an encrypted volume to replace the old one.",
      "EBS does not support turning on encryption for an existing volume.",
      "Encryption by default applies only to new volumes and snapshot copies.",
      "Changing the volume type does not encrypt the volume."] },

{ id: "d5-20", d: 5,
  q: "A financial company must keep trade records in Amazon S3 for 7 years in a write once, read many (WORM) model. No user, including the root user, can be able to delete or overwrite the records during the retention period.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Turn on S3 Object Lock. Apply a 7-year retention period in compliance mode.",
      "Turn on S3 Object Lock. Apply a 7-year retention period in governance mode.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning, and add a bucket policy that denies object deletion to all users.",
      "Turn on MFA delete for the bucket. Give the MFA device to the compliance officer."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["In compliance mode, no user, including the root user, can overwrite or delete a protected object version or shorten its retention until the period ends.",
      "Users with the s3:BypassGovernanceRetention permission can delete objects in governance mode.",
      "A bucket policy can be changed by an administrator, so it does not give WORM protection.",
      "MFA delete adds a check on deletion but does not stop the root user with the device."] },

{ id: "d5-21", d: 5,
  q: "A company archives records in an Amazon S3 Glacier vault. A regulation requires a policy that denies archive deletion for 10 years, and the policy itself must not be changeable after it is applied.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create an S3 Glacier Vault Lock policy that denies deletion for archives younger than 10 years, and complete the lock.",
      "Attach a vault access policy that denies deletion. Remove the permission to change the access policy from all of the admins.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning for the vault. Add a lifecycle rule that keeps every version of every archive for 10 years.",
      "Encrypt the archives with an AWS KMS key. Schedule the deletion of the key 10 years after the archives are written."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A Vault Lock policy enforces compliance controls such as WORM. After the lock is completed, the policy becomes immutable and cannot be changed.",
      "A vault access policy can be changed by anyone who gets the permission back, so it is not immutable.",
      "Glacier vaults do not support S3 Versioning or S3 Lifecycle rules.",
      "Deleting a key does not prevent archive deletion, and it would make the data unreadable."] },

{ id: "d5-22", d: 5,
  q: "A company must ensure that only code that its build system has approved and signed can be deployed to its AWS Lambda functions. Deployments of unsigned or changed code packages must fail.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create an AWS Signer signing profile and a Lambda code signing configuration with the untrusted artifact policy set to Enforce.",
      "Store the code packages in an S3 bucket with versioning. Allow only the build system's role to upload packages there.",
      "Run Amazon Inspector Lambda code scanning on each function after each deployment, and roll back functions with findings.",
      "Turn on CloudTrail data events for Lambda. Alert the team when a deployment comes from a principal that is unknown."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Lambda code signing checks that code was signed by an allowed Signer profile and was not changed. With the untrusted artifact policy set to Enforce, Lambda rejects deployments that fail the check.",
      "Limiting uploads helps, but Lambda does not verify that the code came from the build system or is unchanged.",
      "Scanning after deployment finds vulnerabilities, not unsigned code, and the code is already deployed.",
      "Alerts after the deployment do not block it."] },

{ id: "d5-23", d: 5,
  q: "A company uploads large data files to Amazon S3. A compliance rule requires proof that each object stored in S3 is exactly what the client sent, by using a SHA-256 checksum that S3 checks during upload.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Upload with the SHA-256 checksum algorithm to make sure that S3 calculates and validates the checksum and stores it with the object.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning to make sure that S3 keeps every version of the object and compares each new version with the older versions that it keeps.",
      "Turn on default encryption with SSE-KMS to make sure that the KMS key detects any change to the data that is sent during upload.",
      "Calculate an MD5 hash on the client after the upload. Store each hash in a spreadsheet next to the name of the object."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["S3 supports additional checksum algorithms such as SHA-256. When the client provides the checksum, S3 checks it during the upload, rejects the object if it does not match, and stores the checksum with the object.",
      "Versioning keeps versions but does not check integrity on upload.",
      "Encryption protects confidentiality and does not prove that the data matches what the client sent.",
      "A hash calculated after upload and kept in a spreadsheet is not checked by S3."] },

{ id: "d5-24", d: 5,
  q: "An Amazon S3 bucket receives application logs every day. Logs must stay in S3 Standard for 90 days, then move to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, and must be deleted 7 years after creation, without manual work.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add an S3 Lifecycle rule that transitions objects to Glacier Deep Archive after 90 days and expires them after 7 years.",
      "Run a Lambda function each night that copies old logs to Glacier Deep Archive and deletes the copies after 7 years.",
      "Turn on S3 Intelligent-Tiering for the bucket. Delete the logs by hand when each log becomes 7 years old.",
      "Turn on S3 Object Lock with a 7-year retention period. Apply it in governance mode to the bucket."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["S3 Lifecycle rules transition objects between storage classes and expire them on a schedule, with no code or manual steps.",
      "A custom function is work that a lifecycle rule does for free.",
      "Intelligent-Tiering does not move objects to Deep Archive on a fixed schedule, and manual deletion is not automatic.",
      "Object Lock prevents deletion during the retention period. It does not transition or delete objects."] },

{ id: "d5-25", d: 5,
  q: "A company has an Amazon EFS file system that was created without encryption at rest. A security policy now requires that the data is stored on an encrypted file system, with as little manual copying work as possible.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create a new EFS file system with encryption at rest turned on and a KMS key.",
      "Use AWS DataSync to copy the data from the old file system to the new one.",
      "Turn on encryption at rest in the settings of the existing EFS file system.",
      "Create an EFS file system policy that requires encryption for all new files.",
      "Mount the existing file system with the tls option to encrypt the data at rest."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Encryption at rest for EFS is set when the file system is created and cannot be turned on later, so a new encrypted file system is needed.",
      "DataSync copies the data between file systems as a managed task, which avoids manual copying.",
      "Encryption at rest cannot be turned on for an existing file system.",
      "A file system policy can require TLS for clients but cannot encrypt data at rest.",
      "The tls mount option encrypts data in transit, not data at rest."] },

{ id: "d5-26", d: 5,
  q: "A research team uses an Amazon FSx for Lustre scratch file system to store results that take weeks to compute. The team now needs daily backups of the file system that are kept for 30 days.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Move the data to a persistent FSx for Lustre file system. Turn on automatic daily backups with 30-day retention.",
      "Turn on automatic daily backups for the scratch file system. Set the backup retention period to 30 days.",
      "Take EBS snapshots of the scratch file system's storage each day by using Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning for the scratch file system. Keep each version for 30 days."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Backups are not supported on scratch file systems, which are designed for temporary storage. Persistent file systems support automatic daily backups with a retention period that you set.",
      "Scratch file systems do not support backups.",
      "FSx for Lustre storage is not exposed as EBS volumes that Data Lifecycle Manager can snapshot.",
      "S3 Versioning applies to S3 buckets, not to FSx file systems."] },

{ id: "d5-27", d: 5,
  q: "A company must take snapshots of Amazon EBS volumes that have the tag backup=daily every day, keep them for 14 days, and copy each snapshot to a second Region for disaster recovery. The solution must not require custom code.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager snapshot policy that targets the tag, runs daily, keeps 14 snapshots, and copies them to the second Region.",
      "Write a Lambda function that runs each day, creates snapshots of the tagged volumes, copies them, and deletes the copies after 14 days.",
      "Ask the operations team to create and copy the snapshots each day by hand. Delete each snapshot that is older than 14 days.",
      "Turn on EBS encryption by default in both Regions, which copies new snapshots between the two Regions for disaster recovery."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Data Lifecycle Manager policies create EBS snapshots on a schedule for volumes with target tags, keep them by count or age, and can copy them to other Regions, with no code.",
      "A custom function is the code that the company wants to avoid.",
      "Manual work is error prone and not sustainable.",
      "Encryption by default does not create or copy snapshots."] },

{ id: "d5-28", d: 5,
  q: "A company uses AWS Backup. After a ransomware incident at another company, the security team wants backups that cannot be deleted before their retention ends, even by an administrator with full access to the workload account.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Apply AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode to the backup vault that stores the recovery points.",
      "Copy the recovery points to a vault in a separate backup account in the organization, also locked.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning for the backup vault. Restore deleted recovery points from older versions.",
      "Encrypt the recovery points with the AWS managed key for AWS Backup in the workload account only.",
      "Give the backup vault a name that is hard to guess to hide it from attackers in the account."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["In compliance mode, after the grace time ends, Vault Lock cannot be changed or deleted, and recovery points cannot be deleted before their retention ends, even by the root user.",
      "A copy in a separate account protects the backups if the workload account is compromised, because the attacker's access does not reach the backup account.",
      "Backup vaults are not S3 buckets, and versioning does not apply to them.",
      "Encryption in the same account does not stop an administrator from deleting backups.",
      "Hiding a name is not a security control."] },

{ id: "d5-29", d: 5,
  q: "A company must move 50 TB of files from an on-premises NFS server to Amazon S3 over AWS Direct Connect. The data must be encrypted in transit, and the company must be able to confirm that the data arrived without corruption.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Use AWS DataSync with an agent on premises, a VPC endpoint for the service, and data integrity verification.",
      "Copy the files with a custom script over FTP, and calculate a checksum for each file at the end of the copy.",
      "Ship the files on unencrypted hard drives to AWS. Ask AWS to upload the files to the S3 bucket for them.",
      "Mount the S3 bucket on the NFS server with a third-party file system tool. Copy the files with cp -r."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["DataSync encrypts all network traffic with TLS, can use a VPC endpoint over Direct Connect, and verifies data integrity during and after the transfer.",
      "FTP does not encrypt the data, and a custom script is more work.",
      "Unencrypted drives expose the data during shipping.",
      "A third-party tool adds risk, and cp gives no integrity verification."] },

{ id: "d5-30", d: 5,
  q: "A company replicates an Amazon S3 bucket to a bucket in another Region for disaster recovery. Objects encrypted with SSE-S3 replicate, but objects encrypted with SSE-KMS by using a customer managed key do not replicate.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Turn on replication of KMS-encrypted objects in the rule, set a destination KMS key, and let the role use both keys.",
      "Turn off encryption on the source bucket. Copy the existing objects again to replicate them.",
      "Change the replication rule to copy only objects that have a tag. Tag the SSE-KMS objects for the replication.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning on the destination bucket, which is the only setting that is missing for SSE-KMS objects."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["By default, S3 does not replicate objects encrypted with SSE-KMS. The replication rule must turn on replication of KMS-encrypted objects and name a key in the destination Region, and the replication role needs kms:Decrypt on the source key and kms:Encrypt on the destination key.",
      "Removing encryption weakens data protection.",
      "Tags do not change the SSE-KMS replication requirement.",
      "Versioning is required on both buckets for replication, and SSE-S3 objects already replicate, so it is turned on."] },

{ id: "d5-31", d: 5,
  q: "A company's Amazon S3 bucket must store every object encrypted with SSE-KMS by using one specific customer managed key. Uploads that request a different KMS key, or SSE-S3, must be rejected.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Set the bucket's default encryption to SSE-KMS with the specific customer managed key.",
      "Deny s3:PutObject when x-amz-server-side-encryption-aws-kms-key-id is not that key.",
      "Turn on S3 Bucket Keys, which reject any upload that uses a different encryption key.",
      "Turn on S3 Object Lock to make sure that objects with the wrong key are locked after the upload.",
      "Grant kms:Decrypt on the key to all users of the bucket."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Default encryption applies the key to uploads that do not specify encryption, so they use the right key.",
      "A bucket policy that denies uploads whose KMS key ID header is not the approved key rejects requests that ask for another key or for SSE-S3.",
      "Bucket Keys reduce KMS requests. They do not reject uploads.",
      "Object Lock prevents deletion and does not check encryption keys.",
      "Decrypt permissions do not control which key an upload uses."] },

{ id: "d5-32", d: 5,
  q: "A company stores critical files in a versioned Amazon S3 bucket. The security team wants an extra check so that permanently deleting an object version, or suspending versioning, requires an MFA code from the root user's device.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Turn on MFA delete for the bucket by using the root user's credentials and MFA device.",
      "Add a bucket policy that denies s3:DeleteObject for any user who did not sign in with MFA.",
      "Turn on S3 Object Lock in governance mode. Give the MFA device to the security team.",
      "Turn on S3 Replication to make sure that a deleted version stays in the destination bucket forever."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["MFA delete requires an MFA code to permanently delete an object version or to change the versioning state. Only the root user can turn it on, by using the API or the AWS CLI with the root user's MFA device.",
      "A policy on MFA sign-in does not cover version deletion or versioning changes in the same way.",
      "Governance mode can be bypassed with a permission, and it is not tied to an MFA device.",
      "Replication does not stop deletion in the source bucket and does not need MFA."] },

{ id: "d5-33", d: 5,
  q: "An application calls a third-party payment API with an API key that is stored in AWS Secrets Manager. The provider lets customers create and revoke keys through its own API. The company must rotate the API key every 30 days automatically.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on rotation for the secret with a custom Lambda rotation function that creates a new provider key and revokes the old key.",
      "Turn on managed rotation for the secret, which knows how to create new keys for the third-party payment provider by default.",
      "Store the API key in an encrypted Amazon S3 object instead. Turn on S3 Versioning for the object every 30 days instead.",
      "Ask a team member to create a new key in the provider's portal every 30 days. Paste the new key into the secret by hand."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["For secrets that Secrets Manager does not manage itself, rotation uses a Lambda rotation function. The function can call the provider's API to create the new key, test it, and revoke the old key on the schedule.",
      "Managed rotation is for secrets that other AWS services manage, not third-party API keys.",
      "S3 Versioning does not create new keys.",
      "Manual rotation is error prone and not automatic."] },

{ id: "d5-34", d: 5,
  q: "Secrets Manager rotation fails for a secret. The rotation Lambda function runs in private subnets of a VPC that have no NAT gateway. The function's logs show timeouts when it calls the Secrets Manager API.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Create a Secrets Manager interface VPC endpoint in the function's VPC. Allow HTTPS from the function's security group.",
      "Increase the memory of the rotation function to 10 GB. Increase its timeout to 15 minutes.",
      "Move the secret to a different AWS Region that is closer to the function's VPC. Rotate the secret again in the new Region.",
      "Turn off rotation for the secret. Ask the database team to change the password by hand each month after each review."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The rotation function must reach the Secrets Manager endpoint. In a VPC without internet access, an interface VPC endpoint gives the function a private path to the service.",
      "More memory does not create a network path.",
      "The secret's Region is not the problem, and the function still has no path to the service.",
      "Manual rotation removes the control instead of fixing it."] },

{ id: "d5-35", d: 5,
  q: "A Lambda function reads a database password from AWS Secrets Manager on every invocation. Costs and latency are high at peak traffic, and some calls are throttled. The function must still receive a rotated password soon after rotation.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Add the AWS Parameters and Secrets Lambda Extension to the function, and read the secret through its local cache.",
      "Hardcode the database password in the function's environment variables. Update them each time the secret rotates.",
      "Store the password in a global variable that never expires. Restart the function by hand after every rotation.",
      "Request a higher Secrets Manager quota. Keep calling GetSecretValue on every invocation of the function code."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The Lambda extension caches secrets in the execution environment and refreshes them after a time to live that you set. This reduces API calls, cost, and latency, while rotated values are picked up after the cache expires.",
      "Environment variables expose the password and need manual updates.",
      "A cache that never expires misses rotations unless someone acts by hand.",
      "A higher quota does not reduce cost or latency."] },

{ id: "d5-36", d: 5,
  q: "A company runs an application in us-east-1 with a disaster recovery copy in us-west-2. The application reads credentials from AWS Secrets Manager. The recovery copy must read the same secrets locally in us-west-2, and rotated values must stay in sync.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Replicate the secrets to us-west-2 by using Secrets Manager multi-Region secrets.",
      "Create the same secrets in us-west-2 by hand. Update them after each rotation.",
      "Have the recovery copy call Secrets Manager in us-east-1 during a regional outage.",
      "Export the secrets to an S3 bucket in us-west-2 each night by using a scheduled job."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Multi-Region secrets replicate a secret to other Regions. Replicas are updated when the primary secret changes or rotates, so the recovery copy reads current values locally.",
      "Manual copies drift out of sync after rotations.",
      "Depending on us-east-1 during an outage there defeats the purpose of recovery.",
      "Exporting secrets to S3 creates another copy to protect and is out of date between runs."] },

{ id: "d5-37", d: 5,
  q: "A regulation requires a company to generate its encryption key material in its own on-premises HSM and to use that key material for AWS KMS encryption of AWS resources.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create a KMS key with no key material by choosing the External (Import key material) origin.",
      "Get the wrapping public key and import token, wrap the key material with it. Import it.",
      "Create a KMS key with the default AWS KMS origin. Replace the key material with an upload.",
      "Paste the plaintext key material into the key policy of a new KMS key in the AWS console.",
      "Store the key material in AWS Secrets Manager. Point a new KMS key at that secret."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["A KMS key with the EXTERNAL origin is created without key material, so the company can import its own material.",
      "KMS provides a wrapping public key and an import token. The company encrypts its key material with the wrapping key and imports it with the token, so the material is protected in transit.",
      "Key material in a key with the AWS_KMS origin cannot be replaced.",
      "Key policies control access. They do not hold key material.",
      "KMS keys cannot use key material from a secret."] },

{ id: "d5-38", d: 5,
  q: "A company wants to be able to make a KMS key unusable immediately during a security incident, and then restore the same key later so that data encrypted under it can be decrypted again. Scheduling key deletion is not acceptable because it is permanent.\n\nWhich approach will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use a KMS key with imported key material, delete the key material when needed, and import the same material again later.",
      "Use a KMS key with AWS generated key material. Schedule the key for deletion with a waiting period of only seven days.",
      "Use a KMS key with AWS generated key material. Turn on automatic rotation to make sure that old key material is removed quickly.",
      "Use a KMS key in the default key store. Export the key material to the company's HSM before each security incident."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With imported key material, the company can delete the material on demand, which makes the key unusable at once. Because the company keeps a copy, it can import the same material later and decrypt the data again. AWS generated key material cannot be deleted on demand like this.",
      "Scheduled deletion is permanent after the waiting period, which the company does not accept.",
      "Rotation keeps older key material so that old data can still be decrypted. It does not make the key unusable.",
      "Key material generated in KMS cannot be exported."] },

{ id: "d5-39", d: 5,
  q: "A company's regulator requires that encryption keys for certain workloads are stored and used only in the company's own key manager outside AWS. The company still wants to use AWS KMS integration with services such as Amazon S3.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Create an external key store in AWS KMS that connects through an external key store proxy to the company's key manager.",
      "Create KMS keys with imported key material from the company's key manager. Delete the local copies after the import.",
      "Create an AWS CloudHSM cluster in the company's account. Create a CloudHSM key store in AWS KMS for the workloads.",
      "Use AWS KMS keys with the default key store. Keep a written record of the key IDs in the company's own on-premises vault."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An external key store lets KMS keys use key material that stays in a key manager outside AWS. KMS sends requests through the external key store proxy, so the keys never leave the company's key manager.",
      "Imported key material is stored and used inside KMS, not only in the company's key manager.",
      "CloudHSM runs in AWS, so the keys would be inside AWS.",
      "Default KMS keys are stored in AWS, and a record of IDs does not change that."] },

{ id: "d5-40", d: 5,
  q: "A company's policy requires that the key material of its customer managed AWS KMS keys is rotated every 180 days. Data encrypted under older key material must still be decryptable.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Turn on automatic key rotation for each key. Set the rotation period to 180 days.",
      "Create a new KMS key every 180 days, and re-encrypt all of the data with the new key.",
      "Turn on automatic rotation, and accept that rotation always happens once each year.",
      "Schedule the deletion of each key every 180 days. Create a replacement key then."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Automatic rotation supports a rotation period from 90 to 2,560 days. KMS keeps the older key material, so data encrypted under it can still be decrypted.",
      "Manual key replacement and re-encryption is much more work.",
      "The rotation period can be set, so it is not fixed at one year.",
      "Deleting keys makes data encrypted under them unrecoverable."] },

{ id: "d5-41", d: 5,
  q: "An application encrypts data with AWS KMS in us-east-1 and stores it in a database that is replicated to eu-west-1. During a regional failover, the application in eu-west-1 must decrypt the data without calling AWS KMS in us-east-1.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Use a multi-Region primary key in us-east-1. Create a replica key in eu-west-1 for the application.",
      "Create a separate KMS key in eu-west-1, and decrypt the data there with that key during the failover.",
      "Export the key material from us-east-1. Import it into a new KMS key in eu-west-1 by using a script.",
      "Store the data keys in plaintext in the database. Read them directly in eu-west-1 during a failover."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Multi-Region keys have the same key ID and key material in each Region. Data encrypted with the primary key in us-east-1 can be decrypted with the replica key in eu-west-1, without a cross-Region call.",
      "A different key has different key material and cannot decrypt the data.",
      "Key material generated in KMS cannot be exported.",
      "Plaintext data keys in the database defeat the encryption."] },

{ id: "d5-42", d: 5,
  q: "A customer managed KMS key is used for SSE-KMS on an Amazon S3 bucket. The security team must ensure that principals can use the key only through Amazon S3 in us-east-1, not by calling AWS KMS directly to decrypt data.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Add a kms:ViaService condition with the value s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com to the key policy statement that allows use of the key.",
      "Add an aws:SourceIp condition with the Amazon S3 IP address ranges to the key policy statement that allows use of the key.",
      "Turn on S3 Bucket Keys for the bucket, which blocks direct calls to AWS KMS for any principal that uses the same key.",
      "Remove kms:Decrypt from the key policy to let only Amazon S3 decrypt objects in the bucket on behalf of the users."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The kms:ViaService condition key limits use of a KMS key to requests that come from a named AWS service in a Region. Direct calls to KMS from the principals do not match it.",
      "Requests that S3 makes on a principal's behalf do not come from IP ranges that are reliable to list, and this is not the documented control.",
      "Bucket Keys reduce KMS requests. They do not block direct calls.",
      "S3 needs the principal to have kms:Decrypt to read SSE-KMS objects for that principal."] },

{ id: "d5-43", d: 5,
  q: "A multi-tenant application encrypts each tenant's data with one AWS KMS key. The security team must ensure that a tenant's service role can decrypt only data that was encrypted for that tenant, and must be able to see the tenant for each decryption in AWS CloudTrail.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Pass the tenant ID as encryption context. Use a kms:EncryptionContext condition for the tenant in the role's key permissions.",
      "Create one KMS key for every API call that the application makes. Delete each key after the data is decrypted for the tenant.",
      "Store each tenant's data in a separate AWS Region. Give each tenant's role access to the single KMS key only in its Region.",
      "Add the tenant ID to the name of each encrypted object. Ask the application team to check the name before each decryption."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Encryption context is non-secret data that is bound to the ciphertext. A condition on the tenant's encryption context lets each role decrypt only that tenant's data, and the context appears in CloudTrail for each request.",
      "A key for every call is unmanageable, and deleting keys makes data unrecoverable.",
      "Separate Regions add complexity and do not bind data to a tenant in the key permissions.",
      "Object names and application checks are not enforced by KMS."] },

{ id: "d5-44", d: 5,
  q: "Application logs in Amazon CloudWatch Logs sometimes contain customers' email addresses and credit card numbers. The security team must mask this data in the logs automatically, without changing the applications.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create a CloudWatch Logs data protection policy that uses managed data identifiers for these data types.",
      "Change every application to remove sensitive data from its log messages before writing logs to CloudWatch.",
      "Export the logs to Amazon S3 each night. Run Amazon Macie on the export to delete the sensitive data.",
      "Encrypt the log groups with an AWS KMS key. Give the key only to the security team."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudWatch Logs data protection policies use managed data identifiers to detect sensitive data, such as email addresses and credit card numbers, and mask it in log events, with no application change.",
      "Changing every application is the work that the team wants to avoid.",
      "Macie finds sensitive data in S3 but does not mask data in CloudWatch Logs.",
      "KMS encryption protects data at rest. Authorized readers still see the plaintext."] },

{ id: "d5-45", d: 5,
  q: "An application publishes order messages to an Amazon SNS topic that has several subscribers. Some subscribers must never receive credit card numbers, but the publisher sometimes includes them in messages.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Attach an SNS message data protection policy to the topic that de-identifies credit card numbers in messages.",
      "Ask each subscriber team to delete credit card numbers from the messages after the team receives them.",
      "Encrypt the topic with an AWS KMS key, which removes the credit card numbers before each delivery to subscribers.",
      "Add a subscription filter policy that drops every message that has an attribute named credit card number."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["SNS message data protection policies scan messages for sensitive data such as credit card numbers. They can audit, de-identify by masking or redacting, or deny messages, with no change to the publisher.",
      "Subscribers would still receive the data.",
      "Server-side encryption protects messages at rest and does not remove data.",
      "Filter policies match message attributes, not sensitive data in the body, and would drop whole orders."] },

{ id: "d5-46", d: 5,
  q: "A log group has a CloudWatch Logs data protection policy that masks personal data. During an investigation, an authorized analyst must see the unmasked log events, while other users keep seeing masked data.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Grant the analyst the logs:Unmask permission for the log group. Have the analyst view the events with unmasking.",
      "Delete the data protection policy from the log group. Add the policy back after the analyst's investigation ends.",
      "Grant the analyst kms:Decrypt on the log group's KMS key, which shows the unmasked data in every log event for the group.",
      "Export the log group to Amazon S3. Give the analyst access to the export, which contains the data without masking."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Masked data in CloudWatch Logs can be seen only by principals with the logs:Unmask permission. Granting it to the analyst for the log group keeps the data masked for everyone else.",
      "Removing the policy unmasks the data for all users.",
      "KMS permissions control encryption at rest, not data protection masking.",
      "Exporting creates an extra copy of sensitive data to protect, and it is not the documented way to unmask."] },

{ id: "d5-47", d: 5,
  q: "A company has a private certificate authority in AWS Private Certificate Authority in a shared security account. Application teams in other accounts in the organization must be able to issue private certificates from this CA without access to the security account.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Share the private CA with the organization by using AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM).",
      "Create a separate private CA in every account. Copy the root CA's private key to each CA.",
      "Give every application team an IAM user in the security account that can issue certificates.",
      "Export the CA's private key. Let each team sign its own certificates with OpenSSL locally."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS RAM can share a private CA with other accounts or the whole organization. Teams can then issue certificates from it in their own accounts, without access to the security account.",
      "CA private keys in Private CA cannot be exported or copied, and separate CAs add cost.",
      "IAM users in the security account give the teams access to that account.",
      "The CA private key cannot be exported, and signing outside the CA removes its controls."] },

{ id: "d5-48", d: 5,
  q: "A security engineer requests a public certificate in AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) in eu-west-1 for a website that is served through Amazon CloudFront. The certificate is issued, but it does not appear in the list of certificates when the engineer edits the distribution.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Request or import the certificate in ACM in the US East (N. Virginia) Region, and select it for the distribution.",
      "Export the certificate from ACM in eu-west-1. Upload it to the CloudFront distribution as a custom certificate.",
      "Wait 24 hours, because new ACM certificates take one day before they appear in the list for the CloudFront distribution.",
      "Turn on Origin Shield in eu-west-1 to let CloudFront read the certificate from the Region where it was issued."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["To use an ACM certificate with CloudFront, the certificate must be requested or imported in the US East (N. Virginia) Region.",
      "Public ACM certificates that ACM issued cannot be exported for use like this.",
      "The delay is not the cause. The Region is.",
      "Origin Shield is a caching layer and does not change where CloudFront looks for certificates."] },

{ id: "d5-49", d: 5,
  q: "A company plans to delete a customer managed KMS key that it believes is no longer used. The company must keep the chance to stop the deletion if anything still uses the key, and must be told if the key is used before it is deleted.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Schedule the key deletion with the maximum waiting period of 30 days.",
      "Create a CloudWatch alarm that detects attempts to use the key pending deletion.",
      "Delete the key at once by calling the API. Restore it from AWS Backup if needed.",
      "Turn on automatic key rotation, which deletes the old key after the rotation runs.",
      "Remove the key from the key policy of every other key in the account before deletion."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Key deletion always has a waiting period of 7 to 30 days, during which the deletion can be canceled. The longest period gives the most time to find users of the key.",
      "A key pending deletion cannot be used, so requests fail and CloudTrail records them. An alarm on those events, which the KMS documentation describes, warns the company in time to cancel the deletion.",
      "KMS keys cannot be deleted immediately or restored from AWS Backup.",
      "Rotation does not delete keys, and older key material is kept.",
      "Other keys' policies have nothing to do with this key's deletion."] },

{ id: "d5-50", d: 5,
  q: "A company's key policy for a customer managed KMS key gives one IAM role full access to the key. The security team wants separation of duties so that the people who manage the key cannot use it to decrypt data, and the applications that use the key cannot change or delete it.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Split the key policy into a key administrators statement for management actions and a key users statement for cryptographic actions.",
      "Keep the single role with full access. Turn on CloudTrail to let the security team review every use of the key afterward.",
      "Create two copies of the key, one for the administrators and one for the applications. Keep the two copies in sync each week.",
      "Give the administrators and the applications kms:* in their identity policies. Remove the key policy from the KMS key completely after that."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The default key policy separates key administrators, who can manage the key but not use it for cryptographic operations, from key users, who can use the key but not manage it.",
      "Logging records actions but does not separate the duties.",
      "KMS keys cannot be copied, and two different keys cannot decrypt each other's data.",
      "Every KMS key must have a key policy, and kms:* for everyone removes separation of duties."] },
]);
